SaintD wrote:
> 
> [...] Here is what I have gotten out of this thread, other threads
> and my own experience.
> 
> * Rangers are a fine class for begginers. Elf or Orc rangers might be
> slightly better than Humans.

Rangers are not bad to start with. (I've no distinct opinion on the
latter statement.)

> * MR2 V.S. Displacement: For players who usually die before XL10 it's
> probably too subtle an issue to care about.

I think you mean "MC3 vs. CoDisp"? Especially if you're doing a lot
ranged attacks with Rangers the latter is preferable.

> * Arrow management is somewhat important. Don't shoot things you can
> easily kill in Melee. (Grid bugs top that list)
> * If you are chronically short on arrows, start collecting daggers.

Rangers can do especially well with daggers. You can get more arrows
by #untrap'ing arrow-traps. (Post-quest you'll have got an arrow factory
with your quest artifact.)

> * Finding a hard gem, ring or wand of digging for writing semi-perm
> elbereth's can be a life saver.

Sure.

> * Getting your AC down to -1 is important.

The better the AC, the better your chance to survive.

> * You can Engrave test wands and find the really useful ones that way.
> 
> The orignal question "Are Rangers and Wizards possibly better starting
> classes for begginners than bashy classes?" is still largley
> unanswered.

Don't think so. But they are both fine classes to play. They require
to switch away from pure melee-thinking to make most use of their own
proficiency (casting and ranged attacks, resp.).

> Questions I still have:
> 
> * What are some usefull arrow management strategies and what are the
> plusses and minuses for characters up to about XL10?

Collect all arrows you find, disarm arrow traps, get the arrows from
those orcs and elves (and whoever shoots them) quickly. If you have
collected a bunch enchant and bless them.

> ** Spam everything to get the skillups and switch to daggers until you
> can find more?

Rather concentrate on a class specific skill set; don't enhance skills
that you wouldn't want to foster later. (There are exceptions to that
rile of thumb.)

> ** Save arrows for tougher foes and Floating Eyes and skill up
> daggers?

As long as you don't run into a floating eye you can kill it with any
spare missile or with stones. If you have a blindfold (or reflection)
even bare handed if you like.

> ** Balance arrows and daggers?

For Rangers yes, for Wizards no. (If I understand your question correctly.)
If I see that I get very early a stack of daggers I use them in the first
part of the game, otherwise arrows, in case of Rangers. For Wizards I use
only daggers; they can get an athame (same weapon class) later.

> * How hard should I be working to ID potions and scrolls before XL 10?
> ** Only price ID Identify and only use them for identify?

There are a couple scrolls uniquely price-identifyable (sometime you
have to drop the item more than once to get the "right" value told);
identify, light, enchant weapon, remove curse and enchant armor, then
come the large set of indistinguishable scrolls. If you dig up a scroll
from a niche it's usually teleport. A potion under a nymph cadaver is
very likely a potion of object detection. Dip a worthless item into an
unknown potion to detect polymorph, then dip projectiles to determine
sickness (optionally dip an amethyst, but unlikely you'll have one
early in the game).

> ** Read any uncursed scroll if you are confused?

Do that and you get instant-killed if you happen to have got a specific
scroll. If I feel some urge to waste a scroll I'd read cursed scrolls
while confused, but I prefer to delay reading them until I am sure.

> ** Drink any potion you find on the first 3 levels since you probably
> won't die?

No. (I think some say; "Drink potions in the first three levels, since
you can restart the game if you die.", but that's not what I would do.)

> ** Other stratgies in use by people with high winning percentages as
> rangers?

Use missiles.

> * How important is my pet and leveling my pet?

It is important (generally.) But first priority is to level up yourself,
that's more reliable. In case your pet happen to die at some point you
have the option to tame another one; you can even tame an already large
dog or cat or warhorse, without going through the process of cocker it
up.

